  "dimensions": [
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_f672c0c1",
      "@type": "OverallIntent",
      "instance_name": "overallintent_instance",
      "label": "the overallintent",
      "description": "meme-id: meme_image; hasOverallIntent: Harmful; overall-intent-description: The meme depicts two young children with a caption that sarcastically compares their perceived bad day to the extreme disappointment of not being accepted to the same college. The visual of the children, who appear to be of East Asian descent, is juxtaposed with the text to imply that their emotional state is trivial compared to the stress of college admissions. While not explicitly targeting a specific group with hate, it uses a potentially harmful comparison that trivializes serious academic stress and could be seen as dismissive of the real emotional weight of college rejection. The meme's intent is to elicit laughter through absurd comparison, but it risks minimizing the genuine struggles of students and may unintentionally contribute to a culture that devalues academic achievement or emotional well-being. The image does not contain direct abusive, cyberbullying, or hateful content toward protected characteristics, but the analogy is framed in a way that could be perceived as dismissive or insensitive to the experiences of those facing real academic pressures.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_204c76fd"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_9e540797",
      "@type": "TextualMaterial",
      "instance_name": "top_text",
      "label": "top text overlay",
      "description": "you think your day was bad?",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_204c76fd"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_b9d2dbf4",
      "@type": "VisualMaterial",
      "instance_name": "child",
      "label": "two young children",
      "description": "Two young children, appearing to be of East Asian descent, are shown in profile and facing each other. One child is looking away with a pensive or sad expression, while the other looks directly at the viewer with a stern or disappointed expression. They are dressed in casual clothing, possibly white tops with colorful patterns, and appear to be seated in a chair or car seat. The image is a mirror reflection, showing both children's faces clearly. Their expressions are the central visual focus, conveying a sense of disappointment or sadness.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_204c76fd"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_7256eb9a",
      "@type": "Scene",
      "instance_name": "disappointed_children_scene",
      "label": "Two children displaying disappointment",
      "description": "The scene features two young children, appearing to be of East Asian descent, positioned side-by-side in a mirror reflection. One child is looking away with a pensive or sad expression, while the other looks directly at the viewer with a stern or disappointed expression. Their expressions are the central focus, conveying a shared sense of disappointment or sadness. The spatial arrangement places them in close proximity, facing each other and the viewer, creating a visual contrast in their emotional states.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_204c76fd"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_eb805c3c",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "college_admissions_pressure",
      "label": "College admissions pressure",
      "description": "The meme references the real-world phenomenon of intense emotional and social pressure experienced by students during the college application process, particularly in competitive educational environments. This pressure often leads to significant stress, disappointment, and anxiety upon rejection or failure to gain admission to desired institutions.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_204c76fd"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_0d3536aa",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "model_minority_stereotype",
      "label": "Model minority stereotype",
      "description": "A stereotype that portrays certain racial or ethnic groups, particularly East Asian Americans, as uniformly high-achieving, disciplined, and academically successful. This stereotype is often weaponized to dismiss or minimize the struggles of individuals within those groups, implying their success is expected or that their failures are less significant. The meme implicitly uses this stereotype by juxtaposing the children's expressions with the college rejection scenario, potentially implying that their disappointment is trivial compared to the 'expected' academic success of East Asian students.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_204c76fd"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_1188e771",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "academic_stress_minimization",
      "label": "Academic stress minimization",
      "description": "The meme's core joke relies on the concept of trivializing or minimizing the emotional weight of academic stress, particularly the stress associated with college admissions. It uses the children's expressions as a humorous contrast to suggest that their perceived sadness is insignificant compared to the real-world stress of college rejection, which can be a deeply personal and emotionally charged experience.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_204c76fd"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_b35f1e74",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "college_rejection",
      "label": "College rejection",
      "description": "The real-world event or experience of being denied admission to a college or university. This is a common and often emotionally devastating experience for students and their families, involving significant investment of time, effort, and resources. The meme references this experience as the 'bad day' that the children's expressions are being compared to.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_204c76fd"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_c8b3b9af",
      "@type": "EmotionExpression",
      "instance_name": "disappointment",
      "label": "disappointed emotion",
      "description": "The children display facial expressions conveying disappointment: one child looks away with a pensive or sad expression, while the other looks directly at the viewer with a stern or disappointed expression. These expressions are central to the visual narrative and are used to contrast with the text's implication that their emotional state is trivial compared to college rejection.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_204c76fd"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_c714afc6",
      "@type": "AnalogicalMapping",
      "instance_name": "mapping_1",
      "label": "Children's disappointment mapped to college rejection",
      "description": "The visual depiction of two disappointed children is mapped onto the textual concept of 'not getting accepted to the same college'. The meme uses the children's expressions as a humorous contrast to suggest that their perceived sadness is trivial compared to the real-world stress of college rejection. This mapping relies on the juxtaposition of the visual material (disappointed children) with the textual material (college rejection) to create an analogy where the children represent the emotional state of someone who didn't get accepted to college.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_204c76fd"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_fa98fa22",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_1",
      "label": "User projected onto the children's disappointment",
      "description": "The user is projected onto the visual material of the two disappointed children, as the top text 'you think your day was bad?' directly addresses the viewer, inviting them to compare their own perceived bad day to the children's expressions. The user is thus positioned as the one who might feel their day is bad, and is being humorously contrasted with the children's emotional state.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_204c76fd"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_fcfff5ff",
      "@type": "ToxicityAssessment",
      "instance_name": "toxicity_1",
      "label": "Harmful",
      "description": "The meme trivializes the serious emotional stress of college rejection by comparing it to the perceived sadness of two young children, potentially minimizing the genuine struggles of students and contributing to a culture that devalues academic achievement or emotional well-being.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_204c76fd"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
